  "hypothesis": "Compiler-informed Jev improves fresh contraction schedule choices beyond generic cheap selectors, including a fixed nonlinear predictor, with consistent expected single-call quality across repeated provider responses.",
  "training": "All36 prior compiler-feedback cases, first timing block only, six unique orders each. Targets log(runtime/canonical stock), same42 numeric features. Fit constant,1NN,ridge alpha1,random forest with fixed parameters. No new test outcomes enter training, tuning, feature selection or prompt construction. Prior held-out cases are now training only.",
  "packet": "Unchanged informed compiler-feedback decision packet; no test group labels, timings, paths, replicate ID or answers from other models. Three independent valid responses per identical packet, separately cached and all obtained before any timing pilot. Never retry a valid choice. Record bounded failed attempts.",
  "measurement": "Every fresh case and all seven labels, two independent interleaved11-round blocks. No filtering by model advantage. All choices and fitted policies frozen before timing. No concurrent compilation, tests or model fitting during timing.",
  "primary_success": "In each block separately, expected single-call Jev runtime beats each of constant,stride,nearest_neighbor,ridge,forest beyond max(log1p(.02),3*maximum paired MADlog), positive in at least4of6 structural groups. Group-balanced geometric ratios after arithmetic averaging across three provider-selected runtimes per case and round. No best-of-three selection. Report every comparison and individual replicate.",
  "interpretation": "Success supports middle-tier decision quality on one generated operation/CPU, not useful third-tier routing, application generalization, human maintenance savings or a complete escalation architecture. If a generic cheap predictor matches Jev, report a cheap learned-policy candidate without forcing escalation. Failure or variability is a result, not permission to add handwritten exceptions.",
  "maintenance": "Report selector expression, feature and action counts, fixed hyperparameters, learned labels/model size, prompt, provider calls, and compilation/extraction/fit/inference costs. No adaptation-effort claim.",
  "stop": "Fail closed on correctness or provenance failure; preserve all failures and unfavourable cases. Do not alter cases/features/controls/prompts/success gates after observing fresh timings.",
  "corpus_selection": "Six dimension/padding settings deliberately fixed before any fresh compilation, inference or timing to cover small, square, tall, wide, deep-reduction and shallow-reduction shapes. Values are listed explicitly in cases; not randomly sampled from a deployment population. For each setting, choose four of rrr,rrc,rcr,rcc,crr,crc,ccr,ccc using Python random.Random(20261020).sample in listed setting order. Exact specs are disjoint from all prior experiment manifests.",
  "provider_variability": "Three distinct HTTP requests per packet with identical model/configuration and no local response reuse across replicate slots. Provider-side caching/determinism and statistical independence are not established. Report per-replicate comparisons and choice agreement, not population-level stability or confidence intervals. The arithmetic response average is an empirical three-request estimate.",
  "baseline_scope": "The fixed small random forest is a reproducible nonlinear comparator, not an upper bound on learned-policy quality; no hyperparameter search. A failed superiority gate does not establish equivalence or lack of compiler-information value."
